menu "AWS KVS PutMedia"

    config PUT_MEDIA_UPLOAD_QUEUE_DEPTH
        int "Upload queue depth (buffers)"
        range 32 4096
        default 1024 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S31
        default 128
        help
            Depth of the queue between put_media_enqueue() and the upload task,
            counted in BUFFERS, not frames: the muxer emits two per frame (a
            header and a block), so this is roughly 2 * frames_buffered.

            Size it to span a whole cluster (one GOP) plus margin. A buffer
            dropped because the queue was full corrupts the cluster it belonged
            to, so the cost of an overflow is every frame up to the next key
            frame - not the one buffer that was lost.

            Deeper is not free, in two separate ways:

            - The queue storage is depth * sizeof(mkv_buf_info_t) (24 bytes) of
              INTERNAL RAM, because xQueueCreate() does not allocate from PSRAM.
              1024 buffers therefore costs about 24 KB of internal RAM up front,
              whether or not the queue ever fills.

            - Every queued buffer additionally owns a PSRAM copy of its payload.
              A deep queue that actually fills can hold several seconds of video,
              which is memory and latency both.

            The larger default on ESP32-S31 follows from that target's UVC H.264
            passthrough path: the camera owns bitrate, GOP length and key-frame
            placement there, so video_rate_ctrl cannot throttle the producer when
            the uplink slows down, and the queue is the only thing left to absorb
            a stall. Targets that own the encoder shed load upstream instead, and
            do not need the depth.

            put_media_config_t.uUploadQueueDepth overrides this at runtime; this
            value is what a zero in that field selects.

endmenu
